        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n> \r\n> > If we have a command for downloading plugins based on their source info, I can first download all plugins, and then when they need to be loaded, they will be loaded instantly without users realizing that it is being downloaded/loaded.\r\n> \r\n> I don't think I fully understand the use case you describe. Using the current `add()` will download plugin only one time (per machine configuration) and will add it to runtimepath without downloading afterwards. Do you say that you'd like to have a functionality to even avoid it downloading the first time in exchange of downloading it once during some initial config setup?\r\n\r\nYes that would be what I am looking for. The use case is that, we could set up some plugins to be lazy loaded on filetypes, keystrokes, or user commands. In such cases, if we only have the `add()` API for adding plugins to the rtp, that would mean that these plugins won't be downloaded until they are loaded the very first time. Because these are lazy-loaded plugins, users will not notice that these plugins are not installed until they try to load it through, say, some key bindings. When they type these key bindings, they usually expect it to work immediately. However, we have to bear the initial plugin downloading time. Even worse, sometimes this delay could result in some mysterious nvim error messages.",
